{% comment %}
  Let the mega menu be as tall as its contents.

  The theme caps .mega-menu-container at a viewport-relative height with
  overflow-y:auto, so on shorter screens the WOMEN and MEN panels grow an inner
  scrollbar and the last few links sit below the fold of a dropdown — easy to
  miss entirely, since nothing suggests there is more to see.

  !important is deliberate: the cap is set on the same class this targets, so a
  plain override would depend on which stylesheet loaded last. This is the one
  place it is warranted rather than a habit.

  Trade-off worth knowing: on a very short window a long panel can now run past
  the bottom of the screen. That is the instruction — show the whole menu — and
  it is the lesser problem, because a hidden scrollbar inside a dropdown reads
  as "this is the whole list" when it is not.
{% endcomment %}

<style>
  .sub-menu.mega-menu-container,
  .mega-menu-container {
    max-height: none !important;
    overflow-y: visible !important;
  }
</style>
